  "findings": [
    "FP8-to-FP32 dense throughput must use the datatype-specific metric. Generic tensor pipeline utilization is exactly half that metric here; 46% generic does not mean half the usable FP8a32 throughput.",
    "The large case reports 101.54% datatype-specific sustained peak; locked-base full and one-pass checks still report about 102%. This is a profiler sustained-rate normalization result, not proof of exceeding a physical peak. Preserve raw values and use independently timed MFU against 419 TFLOPS for nominal performance.",
    "Shared wavefront throughput is 32.17/40.09/43.76%, MIO throttle about 2.1-2.4% of normalized active-warp states; these profiles do not show saturation of aggregate shared bandwidth on RTX5090.",
    "Large-case math-pipe throttle is 16.99% of normalized active-warp states; 90.44% of its PC samples are at QMMA. This supports a substantial Tensor Core throughput constraint.",
    "Large-case barrier is 25.33% of normalized active-warp states; 30896/31268 barrier samples sit at EXIT after the final warpgroup BAR.SYNC. Code inspection shows the three unused producer warps wait there while the active producer works. This strongly indicates most barrier samples are idle-warp accounting, not removable critical-path time.",
    "Large-case long-scoreboard is 9.31% of normalized active-warp states; 10352/11304 samples are at branches waiting for SYNCS.PHASECHK in producer/consumer ring waits. Do not attribute all long-scoreboard to global-scale memory latency.",
    "Scale loads use only 2 of 32 bytes per sector (6.25% utilization) because xs[K,M/32] is read as K rows x 2 reduction scales. These are producer-only LDG.U8 operations. L1 hit rates are 92.45-93.75%, so the excessive-sector warning does not establish a proportional end-to-end speedup.",
    "Shared read conflict counts are only 0.13% of read wavefronts in the large case. Stores have roughly 10.26% conflict overhead, but their total traffic is small relative to the GEMM loop. No register spilling was observed.",
    "Medium has 512 tiles over 170 persistent CTAs: two CTAs get four tiles, others three. Square has 1024 (four CTAs get seven vs six); large has 2048 (eight CTAs get thirteen vs twelve). Tail imbalance is a concrete optimization target, especially medium."
  ],
